  "id": "Compound::DB00842 - Gene::2564",
  "verbalization": "Oxazepam, a benzodiazepine, is shown to bind to Gabrq, Gabrr2, or Gabrg3, all of which are subunits of Gaba receptor complexes. These receptors are involved in modulating neuronal activity, and their participation by Gabre suggests a role in the regulation of GABA-mediated signaling. The evidence from the three paths indicates that Oxazepam may interact with Gaba receptor subunits, potentially influencing the function of Gabre, a protein involved in synaptic transmission. The high scores in all three paths suggest a consistent interaction, though the specific subunit targeted may vary. The overlapping involvement of Gabre in the participation of Gaba receptor activity implies a broader role for Oxazepam in modulating GABAergic signaling, possibly through a mechanism that involves the GABA receptor complex. The integration of these findings highlights a potential mechanism by which Oxazepam could influence Gabre function, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the exact nature of this interaction.",
